# Media and time

Local MP4/MOV/MKV when the installed backend can decode them. Pilot priority is MP4. A YouTube URL is provenance, not the file. Do not download a replacement clip or bypass access limits.

Probe with ffprobe. Persist `video_id`, name, SHA-256, size, duration, width/height, rotation, SAR/DAR, codec, nominal/average FPS, time base, and optional source URL. A failed open returns a specific diagnostic error.

**Clock:** seconds from the start of the source video, from decoded PTS plus time base, including the start offset. `frame_index / fps` is allowed only as a labeled fallback on a verified constant-frame-rate file. A fragment that starts at 300s must not restart at timestamp 0. Preview may start at 0 only with `preview_time_map.csv` linking preview time to source time.

Each analyzed frame stores `video_id`, `source_frame_index`, `source_pts`, `time_base`, `source_time_s`, `analysis_index`, width, height. Intervals in SQLite are half-open `[start_s, end_s)`. Reject negative durations, decreasing timestamps, and a second pass over the same source frame. Mark corrupt spans; do not turn them into machine downtime.

**Rate:** default detection grid is 5 Hz and configurable. Keep each chosen frame's real PTS. Pass that timestamp into the tracker. Source FPS, analysis FPS, and export FPS are different numbers.

**Export:** dropping frames must not speed the video up. Five analyzed frames per second are written on that timeline, not as the source 30 fps. A CFR preview may repeat a picture across its display interval; the repeat is not a new observation. A large gap shows «нет наблюдения». Preview duration matches the chosen source interval within one output frame. v0.1 annotated export need not keep audio.

Stream long files from disk inside allowed directories. Do not load the whole clip into RAM. Browser upload is only for a small file; the documented upload cap is 8192 MiB and is not raised without a bound.
